Operator ceiling against order. Observed pileup at kh = 2.55;
the half-spectral rule sits at pi/2 = 1.5708 for every order.

Deployed operator: optimised_coeffs(order, kh_max=2.0,
multistart=True). The Taylor column is the same order without
the optimisation and is shown for contrast only; no sweep ran
with it and Section 4 does not quote it.

order    max k_eff h      at kh   2 sum|c_n|  vs observed       Taylor
4             2.4854     3.1416       2.4854      -0.0646       2.3333
6             2.6276     3.1416       2.6276      +0.0776       2.4833
8             2.7049     3.1416       2.7049      +0.1549       2.5726
10            2.7549     3.1416       2.7549      +0.2049       2.6334
12            2.7757     3.1416       2.7757      +0.2257       2.6781
16            2.7884     3.1416       2.7884      +0.2384       2.7408

The ceiling moves by 0.303 in kh across orders 4 to 16, while the
half-spectral rule does not move at all. The observed pileup is
0.979 above pi/2, which is 86 per cent of the way from pi/2 to
the eighth-order ceiling, so the two hypotheses are far apart in
kh even though the numbers 2.705 and 1.571 look similar on a page.

CONCLUSION. The pileup at kh = 2.55 lies 0.155 below the
eighth-order ceiling and 0.979 above the half-spectral limit. It is
the operator ceiling. A simulation at a different order would move
the pileup by the amounts tabulated above and is the confirming
experiment, but the arithmetic already separates the hypotheses:
pi/2 is not close to 2.55 by any measure.
